Week 5 teaches the program to ”make decisions and repeat tasks”: blocks and com-
pound statements, conditions (if / else if / else, switch), loops (while, for, do-while),
break and continue, and in lecture — also functions, modular programming and scope
(static, register, extern). The lab covers chapter 6 of the textbook: Melody example
(melody from a name), string length calculation in CS50 style, optional do-while and
continue fragments. Below are all the rules, questions, tests, and terms of the week.
3.1 3.1. Key Points (”all rules” of week 5)
Blocks and compound statements
A simple statement ends with a semicolon: z = foo(x + y);. Multiple statements are
combined into a compound statement (block) with curly braces. A block can stand
where a simple statement is expected; it is compiled as a single unit; variables can
be declared inside; a block can be empty {}; after the closing brace, no semicolon is
placed. Blocks can be nested inside each other:
{
int temp = x + y;
z = foo(temp);
{
float temp2 = x * y;
z += bar(temp2);
}
}
[Source: Lecture5, slides 4–6]
Condition in C and the if / else if / else family
Unlike C + + or Java, C89/C90 has no boolean type; in C99 bool is available via
<stdbool.h >. A condition is an expression (or a series of expressions), for example
n < 3 or x < y||z < y. A non-zero expression ⇒the condition is true; the expression
must be numeric (or a pointer):
const char str[] = "some text";
if (str) /* string is not null */
return 0;
if: evaluate the condition; if true — execute the inner statement; otherwise — do
nothing.
else (optional): execute the statement if the condition is false.
else if:
additional alternative paths; conditions are checked in order until the first true one
— if several are true, only the first will execute; equivalent to nested ifs. Any inner
statement can be a block:
if (x % 2) y += x / 2;
if (x % 2 == 0)
y += x/2;
else
y += (x+1)/2;
if (x % 2 == 0) y += x / 2;
else if (x % 4 == 1) y += 2 * ((x + 3) / 4);
else
y += (x + 1) / 2;
