Loops: while, for, do-while
while — the simplest loop: evaluate the body while the condition is true. The condi-
tion is evaluated before the body, so the body may not execute at all:
while (/* condition */)
/* loop body */
for — a ”counting” loop: in parentheses, three expressions separated by semicolons
— initialization (i = 1), condition (i <= n), increment (i++). Expressions can be empty
(empty condition is considered true). Execution order: initialization once; condition
before each body; body; update; condition again. Equivalent to while and a variant
with the comma operator (comma is a low-priority operator, evaluated left to right;
this is not the same as comma between function arguments):
int factorial(int n) {
int i, j = 1;
for (i = 1; i <= n; i++) j *= i;
return j;
}
int factorial(int n) {
int j = 1;
int i = 1; /* initialization */
while (i <= n /* condition */) {
j *= i;
i++; /* increment */
}
return j;
}
int factorial(int n) {
int i, j;
for (i = 1, j = 1; i <= n; j *= i, i++)
;
return j;
}
Laboratory comparison (§6.2.1): both fragments print 1 2 3; the while version de-
clares the counter int i = 1; before the loop, the for version in the header, and this
counter is visible only inside the loop (don’t refer to it after):
int i = 1;
while (i <= 3)
{
printf("%d ", i);
i++;
