break and continue
Sometimes you need to terminate a loop prematurely. break; exits the innermost
loop or switch statement:
char c;
do {
/* loop body */
puts("Keep going? (y/n) ");
c = getchar();
if (c != 'y')
break;
/* other processing */
} while (/* other conditions */);
continue; skips the rest of the innermost loop body and goes to its condition. The
lecture example is GCD with a min macro: if i doesn’t divide a, we go to the next i; if
i divides both a and b — we remember:
#define min(a,b) ((a)<(b)?(a):(b))
int gcd(int a, int b) {
int i, ret = 1, minval = min(a, b);
for (i = 2; i <= minval; i++) {
if (a % i) /* i not divisor of a */
continue;
if (b % i == 0) /* i is divisor of both a and b */
ret = i;
}
return ret;
