Functions, scope, and modules (from lecture 5)
Lecture 5 goes beyond the laboratory and introduces functions: divide and conquer
— breaking the program into simpler subproblems. Example — linear Diophantine
equation ax + by = c: get a, b, c; compute g = gcd(a, b); if c is not a multiple of gcd —
no solutions; run the extended Euclidean algorithm; scale x and y by (c/g); print the
solution. The Euclidean algorithm relies on gcd(a, b) = gcd(b, amodb):
int gcd(int a, int b) {
while (b) { /* if a < b, performs swap */
int temp = b;
b = a % b;
a = temp;
}
return a;
}
Functions return no more than one value: for multiple outputs (extended Euclid re-
turns gcd, x and y), the lecture uses global variables — variables declared outside
functions: they exist for the entire lifetime of the program and are accessible from
any function. A module in C: interface (header file .h)+ implementation (auxiliary
source/object files).
The header euclid.h contains function prototypes and extern
declarations of global variables; extern tells the compiler that the variable is defined
in another file; compile with one command gcc -g -O0 -Wall diophant.c euclid.c -o
diophant.o (both files need to be compiled and linked):
/* ensure included only once */
#ifndef EUCLID_H
#define EUCLID_H
/* global variables (declared in euclid.c) */
extern int x, y;
/* compute gcd */
int gcd(int a, int b);
/* compute g = gcd(a, b) and solve ax+by=g */
int ext_euclid(int a, int b);
#endif
Scope — the region in which a variable is valid; usually this is the block with the
variable declaration. Variables outside functions are global. Lecture example: global
int nmax = 20; and a Fibonacci loop in main; if inside main we declare a local int nmax
= 25;, it shadows the global one — the loop will go to 25 and 25 lines will be printed.
static: outside a function — the variable/function is visible only inside the file (cannot
be extern); inside a function — the variable is local, but it is initialized once when
the program starts and is not reinitialized on calls (static int somePersistentVar = 0;).
register: an explicit request to store the variable in a register; only local variables
and arguments; excess/invalid declarations are ignored; you cannot take the address
of a register variable — a pointer to it is invalid.
