Parameters, arguments, and return value
One function can use another. Parameter — the name of the input inside the function
(count); argument — the value passed by the call (3). count is local to printmeows;
on each call, the value is copied:
void print_meow(void)
{
printf("meow\n");
}
void print_meows(int count)
{
for (int i = 0; i < count; i++)
{
print_meow();
}
}
// calls inside main
print_meows(1);
print_meows(3);
The return value goes back to the caller. The textbook example IntegerRoot — integer
square root: int — result type; number — input parameter; caller — the function that
makes the call (here main). IntegerRoot returns a number; the caller prints it via
printf:
