Arrays, sizeof and address arithmetic
Primitive arrays are implemented in C as a pointer to a block of continuous mem-
ory. arr behaves like a pointer to element 0 — but, unlike a pointer, it cannot be
reassigned:
int arr[8];
int a = arr[0];
int *pa = arr;
/* same as int *pa = &arr;[0]; */
sizeof(): for primitive types/variables — the size of the type in bytes; for primitive
arrays — the size of the entire array in bytes. Array length — is a macro:
