Strings and the string.h library
Strings are stored as arrays of characters terminated by a null byte (last character
== ’\{}0’). Work with strings as with arrays:
char str[] = "This is a string.";
char *pc = str;
*(pc+10) = 'S';
puts(str); /* prints "This is a String." */
Standard header <string.h > functions: copying — strcpy(), strncpy(); comparison —
strcmp(), strncmp(); length — strlen(); concatenation — strcat(), strncat(); character
search — strchr(), strrchr(). Exact slide signatures:
• char *strcpy(strto, strfrom); — copies strfrom to strto
• char *strncpy(strto, strfrom, n); — copies n characters from strfrom to strto
• int strcmp(str1, str2); — compares str1 and str2: 0 if equal; positive if str1 >
str2; negative if str1 < str2
• int strncmp(str1, str2, n); — compares the first n characters of str1 and str2
• int strlen(str); — returns the length of str
• char *strcat(strto, strfrom); — appends strfrom to the end of strto
• char *strncat(strto, strfrom, n); — appends n characters from strfrom to the end
of strto
• char *strchr(str, c); — searches for character c in str: pointer to the first occur-
rence or NULL
• char *strrchr(str, c); — pointer to the last occurrence of c or NULL
