Search and sort: linear, insertion, quick, binary
Basic algorithms make excellent use of pointers; these are just a few examples, not an
algorithms course; complexity is described in Big-O notation. Linear search returns
a pointer to the found element or NULL:
int arr[100]; /* array to search */
int * linear_search(int val) {
int *parr, *parrend = arr + array_length(arr);
for (parr = arr; parr < parrend; parr++) {
if (*parr == val)
return parr;
}
return NULL;
}
Simple insertion sort — O(n²): go through the array until an element is found out of
place; insert it in the correct position; repeat until the end of the array. Split into
two functions:
/* move previous elements down until insertion point reached */
void shift_element(unsigned int i) {
int ivalue;
/* guard against going outside array */
for (ivalue = arr[i]; i && arr[i-1] > ivalue; i--)
arr[i] = arr[i-1]; /* move element down */
arr[i] = ivalue;
/* insert element */
}
/* iterate until out-of-order element found;
shift the element, and continue iterating */
void insertion_sort(void) {
unsigned int i, len = array_length(arr);
for (i = 1; i < len; i++)
if (arr[i] < arr[i-1])
shift_element(i);
}
Fast sorts (shellsort, mergesort, quicksort. . .): quicksort — O(n log n) on average,
O(n²) in the worst case; choose a pivot element; move smaller elements to one side,
larger ones to the other; sort the sides separately (recursive algorithm); implemented
in the standard library as qsort() in stdlib.h. Implementation from K&R:
void quick_sort(unsigned int left, unsigned int right) {
unsigned int i, mid;
int pivot;
if (left >= right)
return; /* nothing to sort */
/* pivot is midpoint; move to left side */
swap(arr + left, arr + (left + right) / 2);
pivot = arr[mid = left];
