/* separate into side < pivot (left+1 to mid)
and side >= pivot (mid+1 to right) */
for (i = left + 1; i <= right; i++)
if (arr[i] < pivot)
swap(arr + ++mid, arr + i);
/* restore pivot position */
swap(arr + left, arr + mid);
/* sort two sides */
if (mid > left)
quick_sort(left, mid - 1);
if (mid < right)
quick_sort(mid + 1, right);
}
quick_sort(0, array_length(arr) - 1);
Discussion of quicksort: in its current form, it’s unstable (equal elements may change
places); sorts in place — especially valuable with limited memory; choice of pivot
affects performance (can be random); divide and conquer, easily parallelizable; re-
cursive — in the worst case, it can overflow the stack on a large array.
Binary search — O(log n) on average and in the worst case: the list must be sorted;
compare with the middle element of the current segment — the new segment is
guaranteed to be half the size (divide and conquer!). Requires random access to the
array’s memory: on sequential data (hard disk), forward-backward search is wasteful
— sometimes linear is better. Implemented in stdlib.h as bsearch():
int * binary_search(int val) {
unsigned int L = 0, R = array_length(arr), M;
while (L < R) {
M = (L + R - 1) / 2;
if (val == arr[M])
return arr + M;
/* found */
else if (val < arr[M])
R = M;
/* in first half */
else
L = M + 1;
/* in second half */
}
return NULL; /* not found */
}
