Recursion: base case, smaller problem, progress
Recursion — a function calls itself for a smaller problem. A cool analogy is ”queue”:
how many people are there from you to the end of the queue, including you? Ask the
next person and wait; the last one answers ”only me — 1”; the answers return, each
adds themselves: 1 + 1 = 2, 2 + 1 = 3, 3 + 1 = 4. The same method, smaller problem,
until one case is solved directly.
Function call suspends the caller: main calls doublevalue(3) and waits; the called
function has its own x = 3; it returns 6; main continues with result = 6. (Returning 6
and printing 6 are different things.)
