int double_value(int x)
{
return 2 * x;
}
// Inside main:
int result = double_value(3);
First recursive function — countdown:
void count_down(int n)
{
if (n == 0)
return;
printf("%i\n", n);
count_down(n - 1);
}
Base case: at 0 — stop this call. Recursive call: same function, smaller input. Tracing
countdown(3): prints 3, 2, 1 (each number on its own line); countdown(0) returns
without printing — 0 is reached but not printed.
Three ingredients of recursion: (1) base case — answer without new call (n == 0); (2)
smaller problem — same function on simpler input (countdown(n - 1)); (3) progress
— each step moves toward the base case (3, 2, 1, 0).
Before or after the recursive call? Printing before the call — values appear on the
way down; printing after — printing waits for the return of the smaller call:
void count_down(int n)
{
if (n == 0)
return;
printf("%i\n", n);
count_down(n - 1);
}
