Answer. p was never assigned — it contains a garbage address, and p = 10 writes to
an unknown address: the program may crash, or it may ”work” — a dangerous case
(in the key test, 10 was printed by pure chance). Fix by key: point p to real memory
— int v; int p = &v; or p = malloc(sizeof(int)):
int v;
int *p = &v;
*p = 10;
printf("%d\n", *p);
