P8. Pass by value
void swap(int a, int b) {
int t = a;
a = b;
b = t;
}
int main(void) {
int x = 1, y = 2;
swap(x, y);
printf("%d %d\n", x, y);
return 0;
}
P8. Why after swap(x, y) are ”1 2” printed, not ”2 1”?
Answer. C passes copies: swap swaps its own local copies of x and y, while the main
variables remain untouched.
