P9. How many bytes does the heap block overflow by and why?
Answer. strlen doesn’t count the terminator: for ”hello”, malloc(strlen(s)) allocates
5 bytes, while strcpy writes 6 (5 letters + ’\{}0’) — the heap block overflows by one
byte. Fix by key: malloc(strlen(s) + 1).
char *s = "hello";
char *copy = malloc(strlen(s) + 1);
strcpy(copy, s);
printf("%s\n", copy);
free(copy);
