P10. Unsigned counter in reverse loop
char s[] = "abc";
for (size_t i = strlen(s) - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
putchar(s[i]);
}
putchar('\n');
P10. Why doesn’t the ”reverse” loop stop after ’a’?
Answer. sizet is an unsigned type: the condition i >= 0 is always true. After i = 0,
the decrement wraps i around to a huge number, and s[i] reads far beyond the array
bounds. Two fixes from the key: a signed counter or a loop with i > 0 and access to
s[i - 1]:
char s[] = "abc";
for (int i = (int)strlen(s) - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
putchar(s[i]);
}
